Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Self-host compiler target regression corpus, version0.6

Normal verification
From the project directory:
review-tests/selfhost-targets/run.sh build/zero

An optional second argument selects the result directory. The script compiles
committed fixtures and checks committed expectations. Historical compilers,
an assembler and a linker are not required. All executable test programs are
Zero; shell scripts only orchestrate processes, files and comparisons.

expected.tsv contains1107 target/fixture pairs:243 exact raw64/EFI64/raw32/
EFI32/real SHA-256 checks,79 hosted acceptance checks, and785 rejections.
Rejections require status1 and empty stdout. Five safe hosted programs execute
string escapes, stdin/stdout and full-u64 arithmetic/memory tests.
Exact-output fixtures also require the actual maximum file size, including
hosted targets:131072 bytes, or16384 for real. EFI additionally requires a
130048-byte .text section before headers, padding and relocations.
Raw, EFI and real binaries are not executed by run.sh.

Version0.6 oracle migration
The0.5 oracle, its provenance and original output-limit fixtures are preserved
under archive/0.5.0/. The new hashes were accepted only after the independent
Zero auditor audit-abi-0.6.zero compared all322 accepted outputs from pinned
old/new compilers. It allows only the exact reviewed prologue insertion,
x64 foreign-call preservation instructions, and independently recomputed
ELF/PE sizes and zero padding. Real-mode outputs remain completely identical.
Of243 non-host hashes,192 intentionally change and51 real-mode hashes remain.

This migration includes hosted binaries: both0.5 and0.6 already use a1MiB host
arena, so there is no arena-size exception in this comparison. Normal run.sh
retains79 host acceptance checks plus the five native runtime checks; the
one-time migration independently proves normalized host output parity too.

ABI-MIGRATION-0.6.txt documents every allowed byte change, the analytically
adjusted exact/+1 output ceilings, reference hashes, and the auditor limits.
Seven deliberate corruption controls demonstrate that unrelated body,
prologue, foreign-call, metadata and padding changes are rejected.

Optional reviewed migration recapture:
review-tests/selfhost-targets/record-baseline.sh OLD_0.5_COMPILER NEW_0.6_COMPILER RESULTS

This command requires the exact documented compiler SHA-256 hashes, validates
all1107 pairs and all ceiling equations, and only then replaces expected.tsv.
It does not build or obtain either reference. Do not regenerate an oracle from
an unreviewed candidate to make failures disappear.

Fixture groups
positive/ covers grammar, each original builtin, nested calls, target-specific
names and u64/real-mode probes. invalid/ covers malformed arities, unsupported
forms and grammar failures. boundary/ covers lengths, numeric widths, operator
chains, nesting, symbol counts and exact output ceilings. cases.tsv and
boundaries.tsv preserve the original target/case classification.

The unified-language namespace deliberately rejects23 formerly accepted
cross-target names: @bios, @protectedjump and @enable outside real, and @call7
outside IA32. semantic-overrides.tsv documents these0.5 changes; they remain
explicit rejection cases. New threading/video operations have separate tests.

Optional IA32 VM execution:
review-tests/selfhost-targets/run-ia32-vm.sh build/zero build/zero.iso

This compiles the u64 probe with --raw32 and patches only a disposable COPY of
the17121280-byte optical image, kernel32 slot LBA104. No persistent data disk
is attached. QEMU runs with long mode and PAE disabled. Success requires exact
ABCDEFGHIJKLMN0123OP plus LF and the expected ISA debug-exit status.
The probe covers carry/borrow, wrapping math, cross-half multiplication,
negation, unsigned comparisons, high-only truth, memory widths, strings,
loops and entry arguments. This is test instrumentation, not an OS syscall.

ZERO_QEMU, ZERO_QEMU_DATA and ZERO_BIOS_ROM select test tools; the workspace's
extracted QEMU tree is detected as a fallback. ZERO_TEST_TIMEOUT and
ZERO_VM_TIMEOUT adjust bounded execution time. VM success does not establish
physical-hardware or native VirtualBox compatibility.
